/**

@page page_exahype_multicore Tasking

\tableofcontents

ExaHyPE uses two complementary task graphs. Peano decomposes each rank's mesh
traversal into subtree tasks, while task-enabled solvers can submit numerical
work independently through tarch::tasking.

# Traversal tasks

Peano derives subtree tasks from the local spawn-level blocks and heavy refined
subtrees. Every task owns an observer clone and private interface copies. The
Grid Snapshot reports their count, the cells per task and the thread ceiling.

Generated data containers participate only when they support subtree-task
replication. Particle sets make the corresponding rank traversal serial, while
ordinary cell, face, and vertex patch data uses the parallel copy-and-merge
protocol.

# Solver tasks

Task-enabled ExaHyPE solvers submit patch or kernel work through the interfaces
described on @ref page_tarch_tasking. A solver task can carry incoming and
outgoing dependency tokens so later mesh events wait for the produced data.

Solver tasks complement the traversal graph when a small number of expensive
patches or kernels would otherwise dominate one subtree task. Their lifetime
and dependencies must preserve the data ownership of the action set that
creates them.

# Choosing granularity

Small tasks spend a large fraction of time in scheduling and dependency
management. Large tasks reduce the ready work available to other threads.
Profiles, `Cells per Task`, and the phase report line `Utilisation` distinguish
these cases.

@ref page_exahype_performance_optimisation provides solver-specific guidance,
and @ref page_peano_performance_optimisation covers the traversal graph.

*/
